rturbations of the function. The differentiation along these transformations is simplified thanks to the known properties of the log-Laplace transform. Moreover, we show that achieving such an analogous result requires the consideration of the suitable version of the isotropic constant, notably the one incorporating the entropy. Finally, an investigation into the equivalences between the functional and geometrical strong forms of the slicing conjecture is provided.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of the Shannon–Stam inequality via the Föllmer process | Probability Theory and Related Fields | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00440-020-00967-w)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: last week; We prove stability estimates for the Shannon–Stam inequality (also known as the entropy-power inequality) for log-concave random vectors in terms of entropy and transportation distance.In particular, we give the first stability estimate for general log-concave random vectors in the following form: for log-concave random vectors \\(X,Y \\in {\\mathbb {R}}^d\\), the deficit in the Shannon–Stam inequality is bounded from below by the expression\n\n# Stability of the Shannon–Stam inequality via the Föllmer process\n\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 11 March 2020\n\n  * Volume 177, pages 891–922 (2020)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nStability of the Shannon–Stam inequality via the Föllmer process\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Ronen Eldan^{1} &\n  * Dan Mikulincer^{1} \n\n  * 2699 Accesses\n\n  * 13 Citations\n\n  * 1 Altmetric\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove stability estimates for the Shannon–Stam inequality (also known as the entropy-power inequality) for log-concave random vectors in terms of entropy and transportation distance. In particular, we give the first stability estimate for general log-con