 probability density\nfunctions f on Rℓ. For comprehensive background, consult for instance Chapter\n4--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConditions for equality and stability in Shannon's and Tao's entropy power inequalities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14021)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Conditions for equality and stability in Shannon's and Tao's entropy power inequalities ... The proof for the first stability result is based on a compactness argument, while the proof of the second uses the Cheeger inequality and leverages concentration properties of discrete log-concave distributions.\nTitle: Conditions for equality and stability in Shannon's and Tao's entropy power inequalities\nAuthors: Lampros Gavalakis, Ioannis Kontoyiannis\nDate: Wed Sep 17 14:26:41 2025\n\nWe show that there is equality in Shannon's Entropy Power Inequality (EPI) if and only if the random variables involved are Gaussian, assuming nothing beyond the existence of differential entropies. This is done by justifying de Bruijn's identity without a second moment assumption. Part of the proof also relies on a re-examination of an example of Bobkov and Chistyakov (2015), which shows that there exists a random variable $X$ with finite differential entropy $h(X),$ such that $h(X+Y) = \\infty$ for any independent random variable $Y$ with finite entropy. We prove that either $X$ has this property, or $h(X+Y)$ is finite for any independent $Y$ that does not have this property. Using this, we prove the continuity of $t \\mapsto h(X+\\sqrt{t}Z)$ at $t=0$, where $Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ is independent of $X$, under minimal assumptions. We then establish two stability results: A qualitative stability result for Shannon's EPI in terms of weak convergence under very mild moment conditions, and a quantitative stability result in Tao's discrete analogue of the EPI under log-concavity. The proof for the first stability result is based on a compactness argument, while the proof of the second uses the Cheeger inequality and leverages concentration properties of discrete log-concave distributions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the quadratic equations for odeco tensors (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/2206.01521)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # On the quadratic equations for odeco tensors ... Furthermore, in the open subset where the linear span of the slices of the tensor contains an invertible matrix, we show that Robeva's equations cut out the limits of odeco tensors for dimension $n \\leq 13$n≤13, and not for $n \\geq 14$n≥14 on.\n\n# On the quadratic equations for odeco tensors\n\nPublished 3 Jun 2022 in math.AG | (2206.01521v1)\n\nAbstract: Elina Robeva discovered quadratic equations satisfied by orthogonally decomposable (\"odeco\") tensors. Boralevi-Draisma-Horobe\\c{t}-Robeva then proved that, over the real numbers, these equations characterise odeco tensors. This raises the question to what extent they also characterise the Zariski-closure of the set of odeco tensors over the complex numbers. In the current paper we restrict ourselves to symmetric tensors of order three, i.e., of format $n \\times n \\times n$n×n×n. By providing an explicit counterexample to one of Robeva's conjectures, we show that for $n \\geq 12$n≥12, these equations do not suffice. Furthermore, in the open subset where the linear span of the slices of the tensor contains an invertible matrix, we show that Robeva's equations cut out the limits of odeco tensors for dimension $n \\leq 13$n≤13, and not for $n \\geq 14$n≥14 on. To this end, we show that Robeva's equations essentially capture the Gorenstein locus in the Hilbert scheme of $n$n points and we use work by Casnati-Jelisiejew-Notari on the (ir)reducibility of this locus.\n\nAbstract PDF Upgrade to Chat\n\nAuthors (3)\n\n  1. Benjamin Biaggi \n  2. Jan Draisma \n  3. Tim Seynnaeve --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematika: Volume 62 - Issue 1 | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematika/issue/CFBC35712F7AAC77F86E3C70AFBF2BB7)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * ### A SIMPLICIAL POLYTOPE THAT MAXIMIZES THE ISOTROPIC CONSTANT MUST BE A SIMPLEX\n[Input]\n\n  * ### A SIMPLICIAL POLYTOPE THAT MAXIMIZES THE ISOTROPIC CONSTANT MUST BE A SIMPLEX\n\n  * Part of:\n    * General convexity\n    * Metric geometry\n    * Optimality conditions\n  * Luis Rademacher\n  * Published online by Cambridge University Press:\n    22 May 2015, pp. 307-320\n  *     * Article\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n      *     * Export citation\n  * View abstract\n\nThe isotropic constant Image$L_{K}$ is an affine-invariant measure of the spread of a convex body Image$K$. For a Image$d$-dimensional convex body Image$K$, Image$L_{K}$ can be defined by Image$L_{K}^{2d}=\\det (A(K))/(\\text{vol}(K))^{2}$, where Image$A(K)$ is the covariance matrix of the uniform distribution on Image$K$. It is an open problem to find a tight asymptotic upper bound of the isotropic constant as a function of the dimension. It has been conjectured that there is a universal constant upper bound. The conjecture is known to be true for several families of bodies, in particular, highly symmetric bodies such as bodies having an unconditional basis. It is also known that maximizers cannot be smooth. In this work we study bodies that are neither smooth nor highly symmetric by showing progress towards reducing to a highly symmetric case among non-smooth bodies. More precisely, we study the set of maximizers among simplicial polytopes and we show that if a simplicial polytope Image$K$ is a maximizer of the isotropic constant among Image$d$-dimensional convex bodies, then when Image$K$ is put in isotropic position it is symmetric around any hyperplane spanned by a Image$(d-2)$-dimensional face and the origin. By a result of Campi, Colesanti and Gronchi, this implies that a simplicial polytope that maximizes the isotropic constant must be a simplex.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of the Shannon-Stam inequality via the Föllmer process (https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.07140)\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; We prove stability estimates for the Shannon-Stam inequality (also known as the entropy-power inequality) for log-concave random vectors in terms of entropy and transportation distance.In particular, we give the first stability estimate for general log-concave random vectors in the following form: for log-concave random vectors $X,Y \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$, the deficit in the Shannon-Stam inequality is bounded from below by the expression $$ C \\left(\\mathrm{D}\\left(X||G\\right) + \\mathrm{D}\\left(Y||G\\right)\\right), $$ where $\\mathrm{D}\\left( \\cdot ~ ||G\\right)$ denotes the relative entropy with respect to the standard Gaussian and the constant $C$ depends only on the covariance structures and the spectral gaps of $X$ and $Y$.\nTitle: Stability of the Shannon-Stam inequality via the Föllmer process\nAuthors: Ronen Eldan, Dan Mikulincer\nDate: Sun Mar 17 17:59:05 2019\n\nWe prove stability estimates for the Shannon-Stam inequality (also known as the entropy-power inequality) for log-concave random vectors in terms of entropy and transportation distance. In particular, we give the first stability estimate for general log-concave random vectors in the following form: for log-concave random vectors $X,Y \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$, the deficit in the Shannon-Stam inequality is bounded from below by the expression $$ C \\left(\\mathrm{D}\\left(X||G\\right) + \\mathrm{D}\\left(Y||G\\right)\\right), $$ where $\\mathrm{D}\\left( \\cdot ~ ||G\\right)$ denotes the relative entropy with respect to the standard Gaussian and the constant $C$ depends only on the covariance structures and the spectral gaps of $X$ and $Y$. In the case of uniformly log-concave vectors our analysis gives dimension-free bounds. Our proofs are based on a new approach which uses an entropy-minimizing process from stochastic control theory.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRend. Istit. Mat. Univ. Trieste (https://www.openstarts.units.it/bitstreams/d3580b52-c220-4b6d-ae3a-344fef819c78/download)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; ON THE QUADRATIC EQUATIONS FOR ODECO TENSORS ... On the other hand, the results of [1] imply that the set of\nON THE QUADRATIC EQUATIONS FOR ODECO TENSORS\n(7 of 22)\n2.5. The main question\nBy Proposition 2.6, X(V ) contains weakly odeco tensors, hence in particular\nthe variety Y (V ). On the other hand, the results of [1] imply that the set of\nreal points of X(V ) equals Y (VR). This raises the following question.\nQuestion 2.9. For which dimensions n = dim(V ) is X(V ) equal to Y (V )?\nOur partial answer to this question is as follows.\nTheorem 2.10. For n = dim(V ) ≤3, X(V ) equals Y (V ). For n ≥12, we\nhave Y (V ) ⊊X(V ).\nProof. See Section 8.\nIn fact, the result for n ≤3 is due to Robeva [11, Figure 2]. Our contribution\nis a counterexample to [11, Conjecture 3.2] for n = 12.\n2.6. The existence of a unit\nThe answer in Theorem 2.10 is unsatisfactory because of the large interval of\ndimensions n = dim(V ) for which we do not know whether X(V ) is strictly\nlarger than Y (V ).\nHowever, in a certain open subset of S3V , we do know\nprecisely where the two stop being equal.\nLemma 2.11. The condition on T ∈X(V ) that µT has a multiplicative unit\nelement is equivalent to the condition that there exists a x ∈V such that the\nmultiplication map Lx : v 7→µT (x, v) is invertible. This is an open condition\non T.\nIn the case of ordinary tensors, the analoguous c